 "perceived_simulation": "[Messages]\n[system]: Ops notice: A320 F-GX?? inbound to E8 has reported a repeat \"SLAT SYS\" ECAM on descent; captain advises this is the third such message in four legs, with no noted change in slat behavior. ETA E8 in 25 minutes, next departure in 55.\n[system]: Library update: OEM has added a Q&amp;A note linked to the leading-edge devices MEL clarification, explaining how to interpret \"recurrent\" intermittent slat indication faults when applying MEL dispatch criteria.\n[Maintenance Supervisor]: D5 went smoothly with your staged package and the verbatim MEL/AMM text—thank you for tightening that up. For E8, we’ve now got a repeat history over multiple sectors, and Ops wants to know if we can still plan around a MEL ground check, or whether this pattern pushes us into full AMM troubleshooting.\n[Maintenance Supervisor]: I’ve forwarded you the last four legs of the tech log and the new OEM Q&amp;A text. Please review those along with AMM 27-88-00 (temp rev), EB-27-088-INT, and the MEL entry so you can decide how the MEL clarification applies here.\n[Line Maintenance Lead]: I can have a senior and a junior tech at E8 about 10–15 minutes after block-in, but being on a remote stand adds some overhead. If this is more than a MEL-style ground check, I’ll need to tell Ops there’s a real chance we won’t make the 55-minute turn.\n[Maintenance Supervisor]: When you send me your proposed E8 package, I need you to (1) cite the key AMM/MEL checks using the text closely, and (2) be explicit about whether you’re planning a MEL-based quick turn, full AMM/EB troubleshooting, or a staged option, with times for a two-person team. Also flag clearly where you’re applying judgment, given the repeat nature of the fault.",
